We need to identify highest-risk bug: Retry wrapper duplicates side effect. In send method, retryExecutor.run executes both invoiceGateway.markSent and emailGateway.sendInvoice. If retryExecutor retries on exception, both actions may be executed multiple times, causing duplicate side effects (e.g., sending invoice email twice, marking invoice sent multiple times). The fix: separate side effects: markSent should be idempotent or should be done after successful email send, and retry should only retry the email sending, not the markSent. Or we should make retry only around emailGateway.sendInvoice, and markSent should be called after retry succeeds, or use a transaction.

Simplest safe fix: change send to call markSent after retry succeeds, and retry only around emailGateway.sendInvoice. Or make retryExecutor.run accept ThrowingRunnable and retry only on specific exceptions. But we need minimal change.

Proposed fix: modify InvoiceNotificationService.send to:

public void send(long invoiceId) {
    try {
        retryExecutor.run(() -> emailGateway.sendInvoice(invoiceId));
        invoiceGateway.markSent(invoiceId);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        // handle or propagate
    }
}

But we need to keep interface RetryExecutor.run(Runnable). Could wrap email send in retry, then after success call markSent. Ensure markSent is not retried.

Thus modify InvoiceNotificationService.java.

Also ensure RetryExecutor.run may still retry on exception from emailGateway; markSent will be called only once after successful email send.

Alternatively, make RetryExecutor.run accept ThrowingRunnable and have retry only for emailGateway.sendInvoice.

But minimal change: adjust send method.

We must not retry invoiceGateway.markSent blindly because it writes side effect (mark as sent). So we must not retry that call.

Thus answer: modify InvoiceNotificationService.java to separate calls.

Also maybe add javadoc.

Provide regression checks: ensure that after fix, only emailGateway.send is retried, and markSent called once.

